GRAPH <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/blob/main/packages/starlayer/starlayer/graph/graph/entailment_regimes.py> {
    ns37:entailment_regimes.py dcterms:identifier "starlayer.graph.graph.entailment_regimes" ;
        a ns2:Module ;
        rdfs:comment """Canonical entailment regime IRIs, from the SPARQL 1.2 Entailment Regimes
spec (https://www.w3.org/TR/sparql12-entailment/) - fetched directly, not
invented. These are the values both ``infer()``'s ``entailment=`` and
``query()``'s own ``entailment=`` accept, and the same IRIs a SHACL shapes
graph's own ``sh:entailment`` triples carry (see
``starlayer.shacl.entailment``), so every layer speaks one vocabulary with
no separate translation table in between.

``ENTAILMENT`` is an ordinary ``rdflib.Namespace``, not individual module-
level constants - ``http://www.w3.org/ns/entailment/`` {RDF, RDFS} both
collide by bare name with ``rdflib.namespace``'s own commonly-imported
``RDF``/``RDFS`` namespace objects, so every real caller needs the
qualified ``ENTAILMENT.RDF``/``ENTAILMENT.RDFS`` form regardless; the two
hyphenated regime names (``OWL-RDF-Based``/``OWL-Direct``) need bracket
access (``ENTAILMENT["OWL-RDF-Based"]``) since a hyphen isn't valid in a
Python attribute name.

``ENTAILMENT["OWL-RDF-Based"]``'s coverage here is the OWL 2 RL *fragment*
of the full OWL 2 RDF-Based Semantics regime (rule-based, via ``owlrl``) -
not the complete regime, which is undecidable in general. Full OWL 2
RDF-Based Semantics and D-Entailment/RIF Core Entailment have no
implementation here and are not planned - see
``docs/future_enhancements.md``.

``ENTAILMENT["OWL-Direct"]`` is the one regime both ``infer()`` and
``query()`` dispatch to for OWL 2 Direct Semantics (tableau) reasoning -
a single name and IRI for a single regime, used uniformly by both methods.

Two values deliberately have no IRI and are not regimes at all: ``None``
(simple entailment / no entailment) and the string ``"native"``
(``query()`` only - delegates entailment to the connected backend/endpoint
rather than naming a regime).""" ;
        rdfs:label "entailment_regimes" ;
        ns2:filePath "packages/starlayer/starlayer/graph/graph/entailment_regimes.py" ;
        ns2:importsModule ns5:__future__, ns5:rdflib .

    ns5:__future__ a ns2:ExternalModule .

    ns5:rdflib a ns2:ExternalModule .
}

GRAPH <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/blob/main/packages/starlayer/starlayer/graph/graph/owl_dl.py> {
    ns37:owl_dl.py dcterms:identifier "starlayer.graph.graph.owl_dl" ;
        a ns2:Module ;
        rdfs:comment """starlayer.graph.graph.owl_dl

OWL 2 Direct Semantics reasoning via owlready2 + Java HermiT - a genuinely
different computational model (tableau-based DL reasoning, with real
disjunctive case-splitting and sound-and-complete consistency checking)
from every other entailment regime StarLayerGraph.infer() supports, all of which run
owlrl's forward-chaining rule engine. See infer()'s own docstring for the
entailment=ENTAILMENT['OWL-Direct'] contract; this module is the bridge that makes it work.

Deliberately HermiT only, never Pellet (owlready2 bundles both behind
sync_reasoner()/sync_reasoner_pellet()): HermiT is LGPL, Pellet is AGPL - a
materially stronger copyleft obligation - and one conformant OWL 2 DL
reasoner is sufficient to cover this regime. sync_reasoner_pellet() is
never called anywhere in this module.

engine="hermit" (this module) is one of two engines infer(entailment=ENTAILMENT['OWL-Direct'])
can dispatch to - see starlayer.graph.graph.owl_dl_rustdl for the other
(engine="rustdl": no JVM, but sound-not-provably-complete rather than
sound-and-complete - a real, permanent gap against this module's own
guarantee, not a bug in either). Both engines' Python packages are optional
extras (`pip install starlayer[hermit]` / `[rustdl]`), not core
dependencies - `owlready2` used to be core, moved to opt-in alongside
`rustdl` for a consistent story across both rather than one being special.
A real Java runtime on PATH is the one prerequisite `pip` alone still can't
satisfy for this engine specifically: owlready2 bundles Java HermiT and
needs a real JVM at runtime to run it - see `_require_java()` below.""" ;
        rdfs:label "owl_dl" ;
        ns2:declares ns40:classify_owl_dl, ns40:_complete_type_closure, ns40:_run_hermit_subprocess, ns40:_require_owlready2, ns40:InconsistentOntologyError, ns40:_require_java ;
        ns2:filePath "packages/starlayer/starlayer/graph/graph/owl_dl.py" ;
        ns2:importsModule ns5:rdflib, ns5:shutil, ns5:__future__, ns5:pathlib, ns5:tempfile .

    ns40:InconsistentOntologyError dcterms:identifier "starlayer.graph.graph.owl_dl.InconsistentOntologyError" ;
        a ns2:Class ;
        rdfs:comment """Raised when entailment=ENTAILMENT['OWL-Direct'] finds self's data logically
inconsistent under OWL 2 DL semantics - e.g. an individual typed as two
classes declared owl:disjointWith each other. Unlike entailment=ENTAILMENT['OWL-RDF-Based']
(whose consistency checking, via owlrl, is partial and silently embeds
a synthetic err:error triple into the result instead of raising - see
infer()'s own docstring), HermiT's check is sound and complete, so an
inconsistency here is surfaced as an unmissable exception rather than
left for the caller to notice on their own.""" ;
        rdfs:label "InconsistentOntologyError" ;
        rdfs:subClassOf ns5:builtins.RuntimeError ;
        ns2:filePath "packages/starlayer/starlayer/graph/graph/owl_dl.py" ;
        ns2:lineEnd 50 ;
        ns2:lineStart 41 .

    ns40:_complete_type_closure dcterms:identifier "starlayer.graph.graph.owl_dl._complete_type_closure" ;
        a ns2:Function ;
        rdfs:comment """HermiT's CLI realization output only reports each individual's
*most specific* known type(s), not every ancestor class up the
hierarchy (confirmed live: for a plain `Manager rdfs:subClassOf
Employee` + `alice a Manager`, HermiT reports `Type(alice, Manager)`
but never `Type(alice, Employee)`, even though the subClassOf edge
itself - `SubClassOf(Manager, Employee)` - is reported and present in
`expanded`). Every other entailment regime infer() supports (all owlrl-backed)
gives the full transitive rdf:type closure, so this step restores that
same guarantee here - walks rdfs:subClassOf transitively from each
individual's reported type(s) and adds every ancestor class as an
additional rdf:type, mutating `expanded` in place. Purely additive:
never removes or changes an existing triple, only adds ones already
implied by what HermiT already reported.""" ;
        rdfs:label "_complete_type_closure" ;
        ns2:calls ns40:_complete_type_closure._ancestors ;
        ns2:declares ns40:_complete_type_closure._ancestors ;
        ns2:filePath "packages/starlayer/starlayer/graph/graph/owl_dl.py" ;
        ns2:hasParameter ns40:_complete_type_closure.param.expanded ;
        ns2:hasReturnValue ns40:_complete_type_closure.returns ;
        ns2:isPrivate true ;
        ns2:lineEnd 237 ;
        ns2:lineStart 202 ;
        ns2:signature "def _complete_type_closure(expanded: Graph) -> None" .

    ns40:_complete_type_closure._ancestors dcterms:identifier "starlayer.graph.graph.owl_dl._complete_type_closure._ancestors" ;
        a ns2:Function ;
