GRAPH <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/blob/main/packages/starlayer/starlayer/graph/query/result_patches.py> {
    ns63:result_patches.py dcterms:identifier "starlayer.graph.query.result_patches" ;
        a ns2:Module ;
        rdfs:comment """Targeted compatibility shim for a confirmed bug in plain rdflib's own
``rdflib.query.Result`` - result *materialization*, a different module and
phase from every other patch in this package (parsing/algebra translation:
``algebra_translator_patches.py``; SPARQL arithmetic: ``operator_patches.py``;
evaluation: ``evaluate_patches.py``). See ``docs/upstream-issues/rdflib-upstream-issues.md``
issue 6 for the full write-up.

Same idempotent apply-once pattern as the other ``*_patches.py`` modules in
this package.""" ;
        rdfs:label "result_patches" ;
        ns2:declares ns71:patch_result_iter_empty_binding_row ;
        ns2:filePath "packages/starlayer/starlayer/graph/query/result_patches.py" ;
        ns2:importsModule ns5:__future__, ns5:rdflib.query .

    ns71:patch_result_iter_empty_binding_row dcterms:identifier "starlayer.graph.query.result_patches.patch_result_iter_empty_binding_row" ;
        a ns2:Function ;
        rdfs:comment """Fix a confirmed rdflib bug: ``Result.__iter__``'s ``SELECT`` handling
guards on ``if b:`` before yielding each row's binding dict - meant to
mean "skip past the end," but an empty dict (``{}``) is also falsy in
Python, so a *real* solution row that happens to have zero variable
bindings is silently dropped too, indistinguishable at this check from
"no more rows."

A zero-variable binding is a real, correct SPARQL solution - it's what
``SELECT *`` over a WHERE pattern with no variables anywhere produces
when it matches (e.g. ``SELECT * WHERE { <a> <b> <c> . }`` - a "does
this exact fact exist?" query). ``Result.bindings`` (the property) and
``len(result)`` both correctly report one row for such a query, and
``ASK`` on the identical pattern correctly returns ``True`` - only
iterating the ``Result`` object (``list(result)``, ``for row in
result``) silently returns nothing. This is purely rdflib's own
client-side bug in ``__iter__``, unrelated to which backend produced
the data - a remote SPARQLStore-backed result carries the same
``{"bindings": [{}]}`` shape and is affected identically.

Fix: yield every row in ``self._bindings``/``self._genbindings``
unconditionally - there is no sentinel value ever placed in either
list to mean "not a real row," so the truthiness check was never
actually distinguishing anything real to begin with.""" ;
        rdfs:label "patch_result_iter_empty_binding_row" ;
        ns2:filePath "packages/starlayer/starlayer/graph/query/result_patches.py" ;
        ns2:hasReturnValue ns71:patch_result_iter_empty_binding_row.returns ;
        ns2:lineEnd 76 ;
        ns2:lineStart 19 ;
        ns2:signature "def patch_result_iter_empty_binding_row() -> bool" .

    ns71:patch_result_iter_empty_binding_row.returns a ns2:ReturnValue ;
        ns2:annotationText "bool" ;
        ns2:hasType ns5:builtins.bool .

    ns5:__future__ a ns2:ExternalModule .

    ns5:builtins.bool dcterms:identifier "builtins.bool" ;
        a ns2:ExternalType ;
        rdfs:label "bool" ;
        rdfs:subClassOf ns2:Type .

    ns5:rdflib.query a ns2:ExternalModule .
}

GRAPH <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/blob/main/packages/starlayer/starlayer/graph/query/sparql_api.py> {
    ns63:sparql_api.py dcterms:identifier "starlayer.graph.query.sparql_api" ;
        a ns2:Module ;
        rdfs:comment """StarLayer-aware wrappers for rdflib SPARQL parse/prepare functions.

These wrappers accept SPARQL 1.2 syntax and return the same rdflib types
the originals do. All four go through ``starlayer.sparql``'s real
grammar/algebra pipeline (``parse12.py``/``lower_rdf11.py``) rather than
text-level rewriting:

  - ``parseQuery``/``parseUpdate`` return a raw parse tree (same shape
    ``rdflib.plugins.sparql.parser.parseQuery``/``parseUpdate`` return),
    via ``starlayer.sparql.parse12.parse_query_12``/``parse_update_12`` -
    genuine ``TripleTermNode`` nodes appear directly in triple-pattern
    position, constructed by the grammar itself, not detected and
    reassembled after the fact from an encoding pattern. ``TripleTermNode``
    is a ``CompValue`` subclass named ``'TripleTerm'`` with
    ``subject``/``predicate``/``object`` keys - the exact shape this
    module's own callers already expect.
  - ``prepareQuery``/``prepareUpdate`` return a compiled, directly
    executable ``Query``/``Update`` object whose algebra is already
    lowered to plain SPARQL 1.1 (``starlayer.sparql.lower_rdf11``'s
    ``query_to_rdf11``/``update_to_rdf11`` + ``rdf11_to_query``/
    ``rdf11_to_update``) - same contract as before ("the compiled algebra
    is SPARQL 1.1; the rewriting is transparent at execution time"), just
    produced by parsing+lowering the real algebra tree instead of
    rewriting text.

Use these instead of the rdflib originals when working with SPARQL 1.2 queries::

    from starlayer.graph.query import parseQuery, prepareQuery, parseUpdate, prepareUpdate

    # All of these now accept SPARQL 1.2 triple-term syntax
    parseQuery("SELECT ?s WHERE { <<( ?s ?p ?o )>> ex:certainty ?c }")
    prepareQuery("SELECT ?s WHERE { <<( ?s ?p ?o )>> ex:certainty ?c }")
    parseUpdate("DELETE WHERE { <<( ?s ?p ?o )>> ?pred ?val }")
    prepareUpdate("DELETE WHERE { <<( ?s ?p ?o )>> ?pred ?val }")""" ;
        rdfs:label "sparql_api" ;
        ns2:declares ns72:parseUpdate, ns72:prepareQuery, ns72:parseQuery, ns72:prepareUpdate, ns72:_check_version_conformance, ns72:processUpdate ;
        ns2:filePath "packages/starlayer/starlayer/graph/query/sparql_api.py" ;
        ns2:importsModule ns5:rdflib.plugins.sparql.sparql, ns5:rdflib.plugins.sparql.parserutils, ns5:__future__, ns5:typing, ns5:pyparsing .

    ns72:_check_version_conformance dcterms:identifier "starlayer.graph.query.sparql_api._check_version_conformance" ;
        a ns2:Function ;
        rdfs:label "_check_version_conformance" ;
