GRAPH <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/blob/main/packages/starlayer/starlayer/shacl/triple_rule.py> {
    ns76:triple_rule.py dcterms:identifier "starlayer.shacl.triple_rule" ;
        a ns2:Module ;
        rdfs:comment """``sh:TripleRule`` as SHACL 1.2 Inference Rules section 11.2 defines it (GitHub issue #82).

pySHACL's ``TripleRule`` insists on a ``sh:subject``, ``sh:predicate`` and ``sh:object`` (``RuntimeError: No sh:subject``) and
writes whatever it computes. The spec says: "Where sh:subject, sh:predicate, or sh:object are absent, use the list consisting
of the current focus node" (the spec's own Examples 11 and 12 leave ``sh:subject`` out), and "Skip ill-formed triples, for
example when a blank node is used as predicate". This replaces ``__init__`` (missing terms default to ``sh:this``) and ``apply``
(an ill-formed triple, a literal as subject or a non-IRI as predicate, is skipped) on pySHACL's class. The expression
evaluator is looked up in pySHACL's module at call time, so the ``shnex:`` patch still takes effect.

Process-global and idempotent, like the other pySHACL patches.""" ;
        rdfs:label "triple_rule" ;
        ns2:declares ns90:_well_formed, ns90:install_triple_rule_patch ;
        ns2:filePath "packages/starlayer/starlayer/shacl/triple_rule.py" ;
        ns2:importsModule ns5:__future__, ns5:typing, ns5:rdflib.namespace, ns5:itertools, ns5:rdflib .

    ns90:_well_formed dcterms:identifier "starlayer.shacl.triple_rule._well_formed" ;
        a ns2:Function ;
        rdfs:comment "A subject is an IRI, blank node or triple term; a predicate is an IRI." ;
        rdfs:label "_well_formed" ;
        ns2:filePath "packages/starlayer/starlayer/shacl/triple_rule.py" ;
        ns2:hasParameter ns90:_well_formed.param.triple ;
