GRAPH <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/blob/main/packages/starlayer/starlayer/sparql/salg/sparql_ontology.py> {
    ns115:sparql_ontology.py dcterms:identifier "starlayer.sparql.salg.sparql_ontology" ;
        a ns2:Module ;
        rdfs:comment """An RDFS ontology (classes/subclasses, properties/subproperties,
domain/range) for the ``salg:`` vocabulary. Not just documentation:
``shapes.py``'s ``validate()`` actually loads this as an ``ont_graph`` and
runs with ``inference=ENTAILMENT.RDFS`` on — real RDFS reasoning over the
data graph before validating it, which is what lets ``GraphPatternShape``/
``ExpressionShape``/``SubSelectShape`` be a single ``sh:class`` check
against an abstract superclass instead of enumerating every concrete
operator/builtin by name. See ``GraphPatternShape``'s own comment in
``shapes.py`` for the full story of why that dispatch mechanism needed
fixing in the first place (a real, previously-undetected gap it caused),
and ``validate()``'s own docstring for how reasoning plugs into it.

The ontology itself lives in ``salg-ontology.ttl``, in the sibling
``starlayer.starontology`` subpackage — a plain, standalone Turtle file,
not a Python string, so it's directly usable by any RDF tool without going
through this module at all. This module is just a thin loader
(``ontology_graph()``) delegating to ``starontology``, plus the generator
(``_generate_expression_class_turtle()``) used to produce that file's
generated section — see the comment inside ``salg-ontology.ttl`` itself for
how to regenerate it if ``expr_families._EXPR_NODE_FAMILY`` changes.

Scope mostly matches ``shapes.py``'s own coverage — Phase 1 core
graph-pattern operators, VALUES/subqueries, triple terms, and all 63
expression builtins — not the wider vocabulary ``to_rdf.py`` can technically
encode but nothing validates yet (property paths, aggregates, Update). The
one deliberate exception: ``salg:Query``'s four form-specific subclasses
(``SelectQuery``/``ConstructQuery``/``AskQuery``/``DescribeQuery``) are all
declared here — a class only needs a place in this hierarchy to be findable
via ``?q a salg:Query`` and RDFS subclass reasoning, not a corresponding
SHACL shape — even though only ``SelectQuery`` has one
(``SelectQueryShape``); the other three are documentation/query-by-type
only until their own shapes are written. The 63 expression-builtin subclass
declarations are *generated* from ``expr_families._EXPR_NODE_FAMILY`` (the
exact same table ``shapes.py`` uses to generate its own per-builtin shapes)
rather than hand-duplicated, so the two files can't silently drift apart —
same discipline used throughout this project's SHACL work.

**A note on ``rdfs:domain``/``rdfs:range`` and why most properties have
neither — two distinct, both real, rules.**

1. *Heterogeneous reuse.* Several ``salg:`` properties (``salg:p``,
   ``salg:expr``, ``salg:subject``/``object``, ``salg:PV``, ``salg:var``,
   ``salg:flags``, ...) are, by this project's own generic-mirror design
   (see ``vocab.py``), reused across structurally unrelated node types
   with genuinely different value types (or, for ``PV``/``var``/
   ``flags``, a small fixed handful of otherwise-unrelated classes
   sharing no other superclass) depending on which node uses them. A
   property reused this way must not declare ``rdfs:domain``/
   ``rdfs:range`` at all — or, worse, declare *multiple* ``rdfs:domain``
   triples on one property hoping it reads as "any of these" — because
   that is actively wrong, not just imprecise: RDFS domain entailment
   fires once per ``rdfs:domain`` triple independently (not as a union),
   so two domain declarations on one property make every use of it get
   typed as *both* classes *simultaneously* (e.g. an ``Extend`` node
   would falsely be entailed ``rdf:type salg:Binding`` too, if
   ``salg:var``'s ``rdfs:domain`` were declared for both).

2. *Entailment defeating the very validation it's meant to assist.* Any
   property whose value (for range) or subject (for domain) is
   *also independently checked* elsewhere via `sh:class`/`sh:node`
   against that same class (or any of its subclasses, since subclass
   entailment always propagates upward) must not have that
   domain/range declared — because RDFS entailment runs *before* SHACL
   validation, it will pre-emptively add the "correct" type to *any*
   value regardless of what it actually is, silently making the SHACL
   check pass unconditionally. For example, giving ``salg:p1``/
   ``salg:p2`` an ``rdfs:range salg:GraphPattern`` declaration would mean
   a deliberately-malformed value with no legitimate operator type at
   all gets auto-entailed ``salg:GraphPattern`` simply for *being* the
   object of a ``salg:p1`` triple — defeating exactly what
   ``GraphPatternShape``'s own ``sh:class salg:GraphPattern`` check
   exists to catch. This rule applies to *every* property feeding into
   an ``sh:class``-checked expression/pattern position (``salg:arg``/
   ``arg1``/``arg2``/``arg3``, ``salg:expr``, ``salg:op``, ``salg:other``,
   ``salg:p1``/``p2``, and several more), not just those two — see each
   property's own comment in ``salg-ontology.ttl`` for the specific
   reasoning. ``salg:triples``' ``rdfs:range rdf:List`` is a safe
   exception: nothing validates ``.triples`` via type-based ``sh:class``
   dispatch (``TriplePatternListShape`` walks the list structurally via
   ``sh:sparql``, never checking ``rdf:type`` at all), so there's no
   check for range entailment to defeat.""" ;
        rdfs:label "sparql_ontology" ;
        ns2:declares ns118:_generate_expression_class_turtle, ns118:ontology_graph ;
        ns2:filePath "packages/starlayer/starlayer/sparql/salg/sparql_ontology.py" ;
        ns2:importsModule ns5:__future__, ns5:rdflib .

    ns118:_generate_expression_class_turtle dcterms:identifier "starlayer.sparql.salg.sparql_ontology._generate_expression_class_turtle" ;
        a ns2:Function ;
        rdfs:comment """Generate the 63 ``rdfs:subClassOf`` declarations linking each real
expression builtin class (``salg:Builtin_STR``, ...) to its family's
intermediate class (``salg:OneArgExpression``, ...).

Generated from ``expr_families._EXPR_NODE_FAMILY`` (the exact table
``shapes.py`` itself generates its per-builtin shapes from) rather than
hand-listed, so the two can't silently drift apart. Used to (re)produce
the generated section of ``salg-ontology.ttl`` - not called by
``ontology_graph()`` itself, which just loads that file directly.""" ;
        rdfs:label "_generate_expression_class_turtle" ;
        ns2:filePath "packages/starlayer/starlayer/sparql/salg/sparql_ontology.py" ;
        ns2:hasReturnValue ns118:_generate_expression_class_turtle.returns ;
        ns2:isPrivate true ;
        ns2:lineEnd 117 ;
        ns2:lineStart 94 ;
        ns2:signature "def _generate_expression_class_turtle() -> str" .

    ns118:_generate_expression_class_turtle.returns a ns2:ReturnValue ;
        rdfs:comment "The generated Turtle declarations, one per line, newline-terminated." ;
        ns2:annotationText "str" ;
        ns2:hasType ns5:builtins.str .

    ns118:ontology_graph dcterms:identifier "starlayer.sparql.salg.sparql_ontology.ontology_graph" ;
        a ns2:Function ;
        rdfs:comment """Build a fresh graph: the RDFS ontology in ``salg-ontology.ttl``
merged with ``spexpr-ontology.ttl`` (lives in the ``starontology``
package).

The merge is required, not cosmetic: ``salg:Expression``/
``salg:Variable``/``salg:Path`` and their subclasses live in the
``spexpr:`` vocabulary (see ``starlayer/sparql/vocab.py``'s own
``SPEXPR`` definition for why), so the RDFS subclass axioms
``ExpressionShape``'s ``sh:class spexpr:Expression`` check depends on
for entailment live there too - without this merge, that entailment
would not fire and every expression/variable/path check this module's
own shapes rely on would fail. ``salg-ontology.ttl`` on its own has no
opinion about expressions/variables/paths at all - entirely
``spexpr:``'s concern.""" ;
        rdfs:label "ontology_graph" ;
        ns2:calls ns111:ontology_graph ;
        ns2:filePath "packages/starlayer/starlayer/sparql/salg/sparql_ontology.py" ;
        ns2:hasReturnValue ns118:ontology_graph.returns ;
        ns2:lineEnd 142 ;
        ns2:lineStart 120 ;
        ns2:signature "def ontology_graph() -> Graph" .

    ns118:ontology_graph.returns a ns2:ReturnValue ;
        rdfs:comment "A fresh ``rdflib.Graph`` combining both ontologies (callers get their own copy, not a shared module-level instance)." ;
        ns2:annotationText "Graph" ;
        ns2:hasType ns5:rdflib.graph.Graph .

    ns5:__future__ a ns2:ExternalModule .

    ns5:builtins.str dcterms:identifier "builtins.str" ;
        a ns2:ExternalType ;
        rdfs:label "str" ;
        rdfs:subClassOf ns2:Type .

    ns5:rdflib a ns2:ExternalModule .

    ns5:rdflib.graph.Graph dcterms:identifier "rdflib.graph.Graph" ;
        a ns2:ExternalType ;
        rdfs:label "Graph" ;
        rdfs:subClassOf ns2:Type .
}

GRAPH <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/blob/main/packages/starlayer/starlayer/sparql/salg/sparql_shapes.py> {
    ns115:sparql_shapes.py dcterms:identifier "starlayer.sparql.salg.sparql_shapes" ;
        a ns2:Module ;
        rdfs:comment """SHACL shapes over the ``salg:`` vocabulary — structural validation for an
algebra graph (LLM-authored or hand-authored) *before* attempting
``from_rdf.rdf_to_query``/``rdf_to_update``.

Scope: core graph-pattern operators (``BGP``/``Filter``/``LeftJoin``/
``Union``/``Extend``/``Minus``/``ServiceGraphPattern``/``Join``/
``ToMultiSet``), the ``TriplePattern``/``TripleTerm``/``Variable``
conventions, all five property path types, ``VALUES``, aggregates/
``GROUP BY`` (all 7 real ``Aggregate_*`` forms), ``Slice``/``Distinct``/
``Reduced``/``OrderBy``, every query form (``SELECT``/``CONSTRUCT``/``ASK``/
``DESCRIBE``), full SPARQL Update (all ten operations), and
``salg:QueryCollection``. Any new operator needs its own shape added the
same way — see ``salg:GraphPatternShape``'s ``sh:or`` list in
``sparql_shapes.ttl`` (loaded below as ``SHAPES_TURTLE``), which is the one
place a new operator's shape must be registered for it to be accepted
inside a nested ``p``/``p1``/``p2`` position. If this note and the shape
inventory below ever disagree, trust the code: grep for
``^salg:[A-Za-z_]*Shape$`` to get the real, current list.

Full expression-tree validation (``Filter``/``LeftJoin``/``Extend``/
``OrderCondition``'s ``expr``) is now done — all 63 of rdflib's expression
builtins (see ``from_rdf._discover_expr_evalfns``) have their own shape,
grouped into reusable argument-signature families; see
``salg:ExpressionShape`` and ``_EXPR_NODE_FAMILY`` below. One deliberate
exception: ``Builtin_EXISTS``/``Builtin_NOTEXISTS``'s ``.graph`` property
is a *raw, untranslated* SPARQL parse-tree fragment (rdflib evaluates
``EXISTS``/``NOT EXISTS`` lazily, translating it on demand at evaluation
time rather than at parse time) — a genuinely different vocabulary from
the translated-algebra shapes everything else here targets. Validating
that separate vocabulary is out of scope for this pass, so ``.graph`` is
checked for cardinality only, same as the still-open gaps above.

**Known limitation, shared with ``GraphPatternShape``: this file uses
``sh:class`` (a plain type-membership check), never ``sh:node``, inside
any dispatch ``sh:or`` list (``GraphPatternShape``, ``ExpressionShape``)
— never the reverse.** ``sh:node`` triggers full recursive shape
conformance and pushes onto pyshacl's own shape-evaluation stack;
``pyshacl``'s shape-recursion guard (``constraints/
constraint_component.py::recursion_triggers``) tracks *shape identity*
in that stack, not (shape, focus-node) pairs, so it cannot distinguish a
true data cycle from a merely-deep-but-finite tree — it can misfire on
very shallow, everyday queries (not just pathologically deep ones) when a
dispatch shape's own alternatives loop back to it via ``sh:node``.
``sh:class`` never touches that stack, so this is safe regardless of
query depth — see ``GraphPatternShape``'s own comment in
``sparql_shapes.ttl`` for the full account.

Unlike ``to_rdf.py``/``from_rdf.py``, these shapes are hand-authored, not
generated by introspecting rdflib — rdflib has no static schema of "which
keys does a BGP CompValue have" to introspect (they're just assembled ad
hoc inside ``algebra.py``'s translate functions), so there's no live
source of truth to mirror mechanically the way ``_discover_expr_evalfns``
mirrors the parser grammar.

**Known limitation: shape-graph recursion.** ``GraphPatternShape`` (the
"what's valid inside a nested ``p``/``p1``/``p2`` position" shape) refers
to itself indirectly through ``FilterShape``/``LeftJoinShape``/
``UnionShape`` — genuine recursion, since SPARQL patterns nest to
arbitrary depth. SHACL Core does not require conforming processors to
support recursive shapes at all (see
https://www.w3.org/TR/shacl/#shapes-recursion), and pyshacl's concrete
behavior is: after ~3 revisits of the *same* shape in a row it silently
stops validating that branch and treats it as conformant (a
``ShapeRecursionWarning``, not an error) rather than raising. In practice
this means a query nesting the same operator (e.g. ``OPTIONAL`` inside
``OPTIONAL`` inside ``OPTIONAL``...) more than ~3 levels deep will not have
its deepest levels structurally checked — a false-negative risk, not a
false-positive one (it can miss a malformed deep node, it won't reject a
well-formed one). ``rdf:List`` traversal (``BGP.triples``) is *not*
subject to this, since ``TriplePatternListShape`` below is a single
SPARQL-based constraint rather than a recursively-self-referential
NodeShape, specifically to avoid this. Revisit if deep operator nesting
turns out to matter in practice - e.g. by moving the operator-alternation
check to a SPARQL-based constraint the same way the list shape was.""" ;
        rdfs:label "sparql_shapes" ;
        ns2:declares ns119:shapes_graph, ns119:validate_query ;
        ns2:filePath "packages/starlayer/starlayer/sparql/salg/sparql_shapes.py" ;
        ns2:importsModule ns5:__future__, ns5:pyshacl.errors, ns5:rdflib, ns5:warnings .

    ns119:shapes_graph dcterms:identifier "starlayer.sparql.salg.sparql_shapes.shapes_graph" ;
        a ns2:Function ;
        rdfs:comment """Build a fresh ``rdflib.Graph`` of the shapes above, merged with
``spexpr_shacl``.

SHACL graphs are mutated by pyshacl during validation, so callers get
their own copy rather than a shared module-level instance. The
``spexpr_shacl`` merge is required, not cosmetic: ``ExpressionShape``/
``VariableShape``/``PathShape`` and their own supporting shapes
(per-builtin body shapes, list helpers) live in their own shared shapes
graph (see ``vocab.py``'s ``SPEXPR`` definition), not in
``sparql_shapes.ttl`` - without this merge, every ``sh:node
salg:ExpressionShape``-style reference this file's own shapes make
would point at nothing at all.""" ;
        rdfs:label "shapes_graph" ;
        ns2:filePath "packages/starlayer/starlayer/sparql/salg/sparql_shapes.py" ;
        ns2:hasReturnValue ns119:shapes_graph.returns ;
        ns2:lineEnd 144 ;
        ns2:lineStart 122 ;
        ns2:signature "def shapes_graph() -> Graph" .

    ns119:shapes_graph.returns a ns2:ReturnValue ;
        rdfs:comment "A fresh ``rdflib.Graph`` combining the hand-authored shapes, the generated per-builtin expression shapes, and ``spexpr_shacl``." ;
        ns2:annotationText "Graph" ;
        ns2:hasType ns5:rdflib.graph.Graph .

    ns119:validate_query dcterms:identifier "starlayer.sparql.salg.sparql_shapes.validate_query" ;
        a ns2:Function ;
        rdfs:comment """Validate data_graph against the shapes above.

Runs with RDFS reasoning enabled (``ont_graph=ontology_graph()``,
``inference=ENTAILMENT.RDFS``) - this is what lets ``GraphPatternShape``/
``ExpressionShape``/``SubSelectShape`` be a single ``sh:class`` check
against an abstract superclass (``salg:GraphPattern``/``salg:Expression``/
``salg:SubSelect``) instead of enumerating every concrete
operator/builtin by name: with reasoning on, a node asserted as e.g.
``salg:Builtin_STR`` is *also* entailed to be ``rdf:type``
``salg:OneArgExpression`` and ``salg:Expression`` (the ontology's own
``rdfs:subClassOf`` chain), so ``sh:class salg:Expression`` alone
matches it. Every individual operator/builtin shape (``BGPShape``,
``Builtin_STRShape``, ...) is unaffected either way - RDFS subclass
reasoning only ever *adds* broader inferred types to an
already-narrowly-typed node, never invents narrower ones, so
``sh:targetClass salg:Builtin_STR`` still fires only for nodes actually
asserted as that type.

Goes through ``starlayer.shacl.validate()``, never bare ``pyshacl`` - this
validates a caller-supplied ``data_graph`` that may be genuine RDF 1.2
content (a ``salg:`` graph can represent real ``TRIPLE()``/``<<( )>>``
query syntax), which only ``starlayer.shacl`` understands correctly - the same
rule ``StarLayerGraph.derive_shape()`` follows.""" ;
        rdfs:label "validate_query" ;
        ns2:calls ns118:ontology_graph, ns119:shapes_graph, ns116:register_salg_native_components ;
        ns2:filePath "packages/starlayer/starlayer/sparql/salg/sparql_shapes.py" ;
        ns2:hasParameter ns119:validate_query.param.data_graph ;
        ns2:hasReturnValue ns119:validate_query.returns ;
        ns2:lineEnd 227 ;
        ns2:lineStart 147 ;
        ns2:signature "def validate_query(data_graph: Graph) -> tuple[bool, Graph, str]" .

    ns119:validate_query.param.data_graph a ns2:Parameter ;
        rdfs:comment "The ``salg:``-encoded algebra graph to validate (e.g. from ``to_rdf.query_to_rdf``, or an LLM-authored ``salg:`` graph not yet decoded)." ;
        rdfs:label "data_graph" ;
        ns2:annotationText "Graph" ;
        ns2:hasType ns5:rdflib.graph.Graph ;
        ns2:parameterKind "positional" ;
        ns2:position 0 .

    ns119:validate_query.returns a ns2:ReturnValue ;
        rdfs:comment "A ``(conforms, results_graph, results_text)`` tuple - kept as a plain tuple rather than returning ``starlayer.shacl``'s own ``ValidationResult`` directly, for backward compatibility with every existing caller of this function." ;
        ns2:annotationText "tuple[bool, Graph, str]" ;
        ns2:hasType ns5:builtins.bool, ns5:builtins.tuple, ns5:builtins.str, ns5:rdflib.graph.Graph .

    ns5:__future__ a ns2:ExternalModule .

    ns5:builtins.bool dcterms:identifier "builtins.bool" ;
        a ns2:ExternalType ;
