GRAPH <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/blob/main/packages/starlayer/starlayer/sparql/ssyn_vocab.py> {
    ns102:ssyn_vocab.py dcterms:identifier "starlayer.sparql.ssyn_vocab" ;
        a ns2:Module ;
        rdfs:comment """The RDF vocabulary for the *readable* syntax-level SPARQL representation
— no more verbose than the SPARQL text itself, built by simplifying the raw
parse tree (``sast:``/``parseQuery()`` output) using rdflib's own real
simplification functions (``algebra.simplifyFilters``, ``algebra
.translatePath``, ``algebra.triples``, ``algebra.translatePName``) — never a
second, hand-rolled text parser. See ``to_ssyn_rdf.py``'s module docstring
for exactly which real rdflib functions do the work.

Design differs from both existing layers on purpose:

- Unlike ``salg:`` (the algebra), a ``WHERE`` clause here is a flat,
  *ordered* list of sibling elements (triple patterns, ``ssyn:Optional``,
  ``ssyn:Filter``, ``ssyn:Bind``, ``ssyn:Union``) in original textual
  order — mirroring ``GroupGraphPatternSub.part``'s own shape in the raw
  parse tree, which is already exactly this, and is *less* nested than the
  algebra's ``LeftJoin``/``Filter`` wrapping (which restructures scope, not
  just relabels it). This is why the projection is built from ``sast:``
  (the parse tree) rather than from ``salg:`` (the algebra) — the flat
  shape is a genuine, mechanical feature of the grammar, not something we
  additionally invent.
- Unlike ``sast:`` (the raw parse tree), path/expression precedence chains
  are pre-collapsed (``algebra.translatePath``/``algebra.simplifyFilters``)
  and prefixed names are pre-resolved to real IRIs
  (``algebra.translatePName``) before encoding — all three reused directly
  from rdflib, not reimplemented.
- Expression trees (``ssyn:Filter``/``ssyn:Bind``'s ``ssyn:expr``) and
  non-trivial property paths (a ``ssyn:TriplePattern``'s ``ssyn:predicate``,
  when it's a real path, not a plain IRI) fall back to the *existing*,
  already-shaped ``salg:`` vocabulary — same boundary principle the earlier
  ``ssyn:`` prototype established: a property points at ``ssyn:`` or
  ``salg:`` depending on how deep this layer models a given position, with
  no new code needed at the boundary (``to_rdf._encode``/``from_rdf
  ._decode``/``to_rdf._encode_path``/``from_rdf._decode_path`` reused
  as-is). Expression names are already close to spec syntax
  (``RelationalExpression``, ``Builtin_STRSTARTS``) and already have full
  SHACL shape coverage, so there is little to gain by re-modeling them here
  too.""" ;
        rdfs:label "ssyn_vocab" ;
        ns2:filePath "packages/starlayer/starlayer/sparql/ssyn_vocab.py" ;
        ns2:importsModule ns5:__future__, ns5:rdflib .

    ns5:__future__ a ns2:ExternalModule .

    ns5:rdflib a ns2:ExternalModule .
}

GRAPH <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/blob/main/packages/starlayer/starlayer/sparql/star_query.py> {
    ns102:star_query.py dcterms:identifier "starlayer.sparql.star_query" ;
        a ns2:Module ;
        rdfs:comment """``StarQuery`` - binds a SPARQL query (prepared once) plus every per-call
option ``StarLayerGraph.query()`` otherwise takes, so the same query can be
run against several different graphs without re-passing the same
``entailment=``/``initNs=``/etc. arguments every time.

Mirrors ``starlayer.shacl.StarShaclSchema``'s own "bind the thing to
interpret, plus cross-cutting config, once at construction" shape -
``StarShaclSchema`` binds a shapes graph; this binds a query instead.
Deliberately does not validate a ``CONSTRUCT`` query's own result graph
against a SHACL shape - considered and rejected as out of scope for this
class.

**A real, pre-existing ``StarLayerGraph.query()`` gap, found while building
this class, not assumed away**: ``entailment=ENTAILMENT.RDF``/``RDFS``
rewrites a query's algebra to express the entailment rule - but that
rewrite only ever runs when ``query_object`` is a plain string (inside
``query_cache.prepare_query_cached``, which ``StarLayerGraph.query()``
only calls on the ``isinstance(query_object, str)`` branch). Hand it an
*already-prepared* ``Query`` object instead (exactly what preparing once
via :func:`starlayer.sparql.parse12.prepare_query_12` and reusing it would
otherwise do) with ``entailment=ENTAILMENT.RDFS`` and the rewrite silently
never happens - confirmed live: the same query returns results against
raw text + ``entailment=ENTAILMENT.RDFS``, and an empty result against a
pre-prepared ``Query`` object + the identical ``entailment=``, no error
either way. Since this class's own purpose is exactly "prepare once, reuse
across calls with a bound entailment regime," silently shipping that bug
through it would make the entailment-binding feature actively wrong for
two of the four real regimes. Worked around here (see :meth:`run`) rather
than fixed at the ``StarLayerGraph.query()`` level - that method's own
algebra-rewrite step mutates a tree in place with no safe, cheap way to
copy an ``rdflib`` ``CompValue`` tree first (confirmed: a plain
``copy.deepcopy`` fails outright, ``CompValue.__init__()`` has no
zero-argument reconstruction path pickle/copy needs), so fixing it
properly there is a larger, riskier change to a heavily-shared method -
out of scope for building this class. Flagged in
``StarLayerGraph.query()``'s own docstring too.""" ;
        rdfs:label "star_query" ;
        ns2:declares ns129:StarQuery ;
        ns2:filePath "packages/starlayer/starlayer/sparql/star_query.py" ;
        ns2:importsModule ns5:__future__, ns5:typing, ns5:collections.abc, ns5:rdflib.query .

    ns129:StarQuery dcterms:identifier "starlayer.sparql.star_query.StarQuery" ;
        a ns2:Class ;
